<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Carries the backup exclusion rules resource, and nothing else.

    It declares no `<application>` attributes on purpose. It did once, and that is the
    mistake this comment exists to stop somebody repeating: the AppsFlyer SDK's manifest
    sets `android:allowBackup="true"` and its own backup rules, two libraries at the same
    level cannot settle a conflict between themselves, and `tools:replace` here does not
    win it. Declaring `fullBackupContent` here failed the build with a colliding
    attributes error; declaring `allowBackup` here merged green and silently kept the
    SDK's `true`, which is worse. The attributes belong to the application module and
    live in `Assets/Plugins/Android/LauncherManifest.xml`, which outranks every library.

    A library's `res` is merged into the application package, so the rules file it ships
    resolves as `@xml/glimmer_data_extraction_rules` from that manifest. Resource merging
    has no priority contest in it, which is why this half works from a library when the
    attribute half does not.

    The package name is a namespace rather than code, and must be unique: AGP 9 refuses
    a namespace shared by two modules.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          package="com.tekoworld.glimmergroove.backup"
          android:versionCode="1"
          android:versionName="1.0">
</manifest>
